{
  "result": "At dawn, the city looks briefly unfinished. Windows catch the pale light, buses sigh at empty stops, and shopkeepers lift their shutters as if opening small theaters. For a few minutes, nobody seems entirely certain what the day will become. That uncertainty is not a problem; it is a kind of room.\n\nThe best ideas often arrive while we are doing something ordinary. A kettle begins to sing, a dog studies a leaf, someone waits for a crossing signal, and the mind quietly rearranges its furniture. We notice a connection we had overlooked, or remember a name that felt lost. Nothing dramatic has happened, yet the world has shifted by a degree.\n\nPerhaps this is why I like walking without a destination. A route chosen only for usefulness can make every block feel like a corridor. Wandering turns streets into invitations. There is a bakery whose window glows warmly, a courtyard full of bicycles, a blue door I have passed a hundred times without seeing.\n\nWe are often told to make every minute count, as though time were a handful of coins leaking through a pocket. But some minutes matter precisely because they produce nothing measurable. They let the mind loosen its grip. They remind us that attention is not merely a tool for getting somewhere; it is also a way of being somewhere.\n\nBy evening, the city has gathered its noise again. Still, I carry a little of the morning\u2019s spaciousness with me, like sunlight lingering behind my ribs.",
